issues.problematic_code_snippet:
/* Validate that stored-key to key conversion works correctly */
static int validateStoredKeyConversion(dict *d, const void *key __stored_key) {
    const void *extracted = dictStoredKey2Key(d, key);
    if (d->type->keyFromStoredKey) {
        return extracted != NULL;
    }
    return extracted == key;
}

/* Before the key is released, obtain the link to
 * ensure we can safely access and update the key. */
const void *key = dictStoredKey2Key(d, ptr);
link = dictFindLink(d, ptr, NULL);
serverAssert(link);

/* Setting key of existing dictEntry (newItem == 0)*/

if (*link == NULL) {
    *link = dictFindLink(d, addedKey, NULL);
    assert(*link != NULL);
}

findings.body:
`activeDefragHfieldAndUpdateRef` now calls `dictStoredKey2Key()`:

::diff{file="src/defrag.c" lines="275-288"}

That function is declared `static` in dict.c (line 97) and isn't declared in dict.h:

::code{file="src/dict.c" lines="97-99"}

I compiled defrag.c with `-DHAVE_DEFRAG`, which is set on the default Linux jemalloc build:

```
defrag.c:280:23: warning: implicit declaration of function 'dictStoredKey2Key'
defrag.c:280:23: warning: initialization of 'const void *' from 'int' makes pointer from integer
defrag.c:280:17: warning: unused variable 'key'
```

::callout{type="warning"}
GCC 14+ and recent Clang reject implicit declarations as an error. Older compilers instead fail at link time, because the symbol has internal linkage in dict.o.

The result isn't even used: `dictFindLink` is still called with `ptr`. For hash dicts no conversion is needed, because `Entry*` is sds-compatible and `entryHashDictType` has no `keyFromStoredKey`. The simplest fix is to delete the line:

::suggestion{file="src/defrag.c" lines="280-282"}
    link = dictFindLink(d, ptr, NULL);
    serverAssert(link);

In the `newItem == 0` path, when the caller passes `*link == NULL`, the code now looks up with `addedKey`:

::diff{file="src/dict.c" lines="911-916"}

`dictFindLink` expects a lookup key. After this PR, `hashFunction` and `keyCompare` only ever receive lookup keys. `addedKey` is the stored key, possibly after `keyDup`. For `dbDictType`/`dbExpiresDictType`, that means a `kvobj*` gets hashed by `dictSdsHash` and compared by `dictSdsCompareKV` as if it were an sds. That reads garbage length bytes and never finds the entry, so `assert(*link != NULL)` fires.

The base code had the same latent issue, since it passed `key` without the stored-key toggle. But the point of this refactor is that dict converts stored keys itself everywhere, and the `newItem == 1` branch just above already does that correctly. Current kvstore callers all pass a non-NULL link, so this is latent. It is still a trap for the next caller who relies on the documented fallback ("If *link is NULL, dictFindLink() will be called").

::suggestion{file="src/dict.c" lines="913-916"}
    if (*link == NULL) {
        *link = dictFindLink(d, dictStoredKey2Key(d, key), NULL);
        assert(*link != NULL);
    }

This helper is never called anywhere:

::diff{file="src/dict.c" lines="101-108"}

Compiling dict.c gives `warning: 'validateStoredKeyConversion' defined but not used [-Wunused-function]`. Its logic is also nearly a tautology: when the callback is NULL it checks `key == key`. Either remove it, or turn it into a `debugAssert` at the insertion points if you want the check.
